%T A402102 13641300,26245875,48033200,84304200,142761460,233578225,369476025,
%U A402102 566998300,847563800,1235297685,1754428500,2432344300,3302160900,4392839025,5719707621,7297032900,9147013200,11270062125,13626478800,16182483980
%N A402102 Number of ways to earn a score of 1.5*n on the AMC 10 or AMC 12, distinct up to the particular questions answered correctly, left blank, or answered incorrectly. See comments.
%C A402102 The AMC 10 and AMC 12 are competition mathematics tests administered annually to high school students in the United States. Both are 75-minute, 25 multiple choice question exams with five choices per question. Since 2007, the scoring system has been as follows: 6 points for every question answered correctly, 1.5 points for every question left blank, and 0 points for every question answered incorrectly.
%C A402102 Since the score earned must be a multiple of 1.5 between 0 and 150, this sequence only lists a(n) for 0 <= n <= 100. For any other value of n, a(n) is zero. The only unattainable scores between 0 and 150 that are multiples of 1.5 are 142.5, 147, and 148.5 (n = 95, 98, 99).
%C A402102 See A402101 for a different approach to counting ways of earning a certain score.
%H A402102 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A402102/b402102.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..100</a>
%H A402102 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A402102/a402102.pdf">Derivation of formula</a>
%H A402102 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Mathematics_Competitions">American Mathematics Competitions</a>
%F A402102 a(n) = Sum_{k=max(0,floor((n+1)/3)-8)..floor(n/4)} 25!/(k!*(n-4k)!*(3k-n+25)!).
%F A402102 G.f.: (1+x+x^4)^25.
%e A402102 G.f. = 1 + 25*x + 300*x^2 + 2300*x^3 + 12675*x^4 + 53730*x^5 + ... + x^100.
%Y A402102 Cf. A402101.
%K A402102 nonn,easy,fini,full,new
%O A402102 0,2
